  "post": {
    "caption": "Madame X's jeweled strap slipped. John Singer Sargent painted Virginie Gautreau in a black satin dress. Jeweled straps held it up, and a diamond crescent crowned her hair. At first, he painted her right strap sliding off her shoulder. At the 1884 Paris Salon, it drew more ridicule than praise. Gautreau and her mother begged him to take it down. He refused, but later painted the strap back on her shoulder. He kept the portrait for decades. He asked that her name be dropped, so she became Madame X. In 1916, he sold it to the Metropolitan Museum of Art.",
